WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.574 --> 00:00:07.486
- [Voiceover] Horses are
a merit to your soul.

00:00:07.486 --> 00:00:11.078
They have feelings, you
know? They understand you.

00:00:11.078 --> 00:00:13.405
And to be able to have that
special bond with a horse,

00:00:13.405 --> 00:00:16.666
I think is very, very special.

00:00:16.666 --> 00:00:20.636
My name's Andrew Lakeman
and I'm a racehorse trainer.

00:00:20.636 --> 00:00:23.918
I think from a very young
age I wanted to be a jockey.

00:00:23.918 --> 00:00:27.025
I was riding as an apprentice in England

00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:28.752
and I got the opportunity to come over

00:00:28.752 --> 00:00:31.188
to the United States to work for a

00:00:31.188 --> 00:00:33.605
very famous trainer in Maryland.

00:00:36.157 --> 00:00:38.057
Unfortunately, in 2007

00:00:38.057 --> 00:00:41.239
I was riding a horse on the grass

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:44.739
at Belmont Park and my horse clipped heels

00:00:45.705 --> 00:00:49.122
and I went down and
had a horrific accident

00:00:50.623 --> 00:00:52.540
which left me paralyzed.

00:00:53.701 --> 00:00:54.821
The doctor was very direct.

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:58.672
He told me that I will never walk again.

00:00:58.672 --> 00:01:01.339
That feeling really hit me then,

00:01:02.618 --> 00:01:04.623
thinking I was never gonna ride again.

00:01:04.623 --> 00:01:07.290
What am I gonna do with my life?

00:01:08.878 --> 00:01:12.211
When I was recovering,
I had in my mind

00:01:13.571 --> 00:01:15.565
I couldn't give up.

00:01:15.565 --> 00:01:18.529
I wanted to find my own independence

00:01:18.529 --> 00:01:20.362
and I wanted to train.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:23.502
That's what I did.

00:01:23.502 --> 00:01:25.113
I bought my own horse,

00:01:25.113 --> 00:01:27.816
I started out with him and I trained him

00:01:27.816 --> 00:01:29.399
and I won with him.

00:01:30.907 --> 00:01:33.055
Working with the horses,
it's kind of like,

00:01:33.055 --> 00:01:35.414
they know that I'm in a wheelchair.

00:01:35.414 --> 00:01:39.032
They kind of sense what's going on.

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:41.049
They would never hurt
me. I can go in the stall

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:43.336
and I can check their leg.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:45.370
You know, a lot of people
would think that they would

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:48.575
jump away from the wheelchair,
they would be afraid.

00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:51.617
But no, they were like,
really understanding

00:01:51.617 --> 00:01:54.284
and caring, you know, around me.

00:01:56.227 --> 00:01:57.835
You know, I'm grateful
for a lot of things,

00:01:57.835 --> 00:02:02.615
but I think the most thing
I'm grateful for are horses

00:02:02.615 --> 00:02:06.591
because they've given me
everything in my life.

00:02:06.591 --> 00:02:10.620
And then after the
accident, they continued to

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.203
give me a new life.

